1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,090
Over the past month or so,

2
00:00:02,090 --> 00:00:04,702
I think many people have been concerned about

3
00:00:04,702 --> 00:00:08,417
the war between the U.S., Israel, and Iran —

4
00:00:08,417 --> 00:00:12,365
a conflict that has shaken the global situation.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,090
Today I'd like to start with this topic,

6
00:00:16,090 --> 00:00:18,005
but from a different angle.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,368
I think war really serves as a kind of stage —

8
00:00:23,368 --> 00:00:26,516
a platform, if you like — where you can see

9
00:00:26,516 --> 00:00:29,224
just how astonishing, how formidable,

10
00:00:29,224 --> 00:00:31,860
human invention can be in this area.

11
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,704
In this war, you see all kinds of different weapons.

12
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,874
I don't know if you've been following the news,

13
00:00:38,874 --> 00:00:42,299
but in the upper-left corner here, that's the MOAB —

14
00:00:42,299 --> 00:00:43,634
the Mother of All Bombs.

15
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:51,117
It's the most powerful bomb in the conventional arsenal.

16
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:59,808
Even just to drop it, they need a C-130 Hercules transport plane,

17
00:00:59,808 --> 00:01:02,188
because it's too heavy and too large.

18
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:06,293
This Mother of All Bombs

19
00:01:06,293 --> 00:01:09,100
is the strongest in the conventional category.

20
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:10,900
If you want to go beyond that,

21
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:14,034
you have to step into unconventional weapons —

22
00:01:14,034 --> 00:01:15,834
nuclear weapons, for example.

23
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,200
That's pushing things to the limit.

24
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:22,870
Next to it is the bunker-buster bomb.

25
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,089
As you know, Iran has many underground missile cities,

26
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:29,411
underground nuclear facilities.

27
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,499
The bomber that drops these bunker busters is the B-2.

28
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,564
And that picture down on the lower left —

29
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:43,672
that one there — that is a torpedo. What you're

30
00:01:43,672 --> 00:01:47,780
looking at is the Mk 48, a heavyweight torpedo.

31
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:50,508
You might still remember, in the Indian Ocean,

32
00:01:50,508 --> 00:01:54,862
an Iranian frigate that had just finished joint exercises with India

33
00:01:54,862 --> 00:01:59,970
was returning to Iran when a U.S. submarine sank it en route.

34
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:07,720
That frigate wasn't a major warship, and it wasn't particularly advanced.

35
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:10,560
A missile would have been enough to take it out.

36
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:14,914
With a missile, you'd at least give the crew a chance to escape.

37
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,280
But they actually used this heavy torpedo.

38
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,550
You can find the footage on YouTube —

39
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:23,360
the ship went down very fast.

40
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:25,798
By the time Sri Lanka's old vessels arrived,

41
00:02:25,798 --> 00:02:28,004
the ship had already sunk.

42
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:31,759
So why use a heavyweight torpedo against it?

43
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,989
The story is that on board the U.S. submarine that day —

44
00:02:39,989 --> 00:02:42,253
a Los Angeles-class sub —

45
00:02:42,253 --> 00:02:45,852
there were two Australian naval officers undergoing training.

46
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,682
You know, Australia bought nuclear submarines from the U.S.,

47
00:02:51,682 --> 00:02:54,410
and in doing so, they tacitly agreed

48
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:58,299
that if anything happens at the first island chain in the Pacific,

49
00:02:58,299 --> 00:03:00,157
Australia would join the fight.

50
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:05,088
So inside that submarine that day,

51
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:08,165
it was effectively combat training as well.

52
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,466
And next to that, another impressive one —

53
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:15,614
it's called the graphite bomb.

54
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:20,419
They threatened to bomb Iran's power plant with it.

55
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,074
Notice — it's not a weapon that harms civilians.

56
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:29,752
Because they noticed one phenomenon:

57
00:03:29,752 --> 00:03:33,803
in some rural areas, in places with very few people,

58
00:03:33,803 --> 00:03:36,440
the electricity usage was surprisingly high.

59
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:39,902
That usually means there are nuclear facilities behind it,

60
00:03:39,902 --> 00:03:41,860
or maybe military installations.

61
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:43,464
So the U.S. demand —

62
00:03:43,464 --> 00:03:45,749
their threat to bomb the power plant —

63
00:03:45,749 --> 00:03:49,492
but if you actually destroyed the plant, that would be terrible:

64
00:03:49,492 --> 00:03:52,360
it would affect many people and civilian life.

65
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,240
So what is this graphite bomb?

66
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:56,318
The moment it's deployed,

67
00:03:56,318 --> 00:03:59,955
the graphite itself causes massive short-circuits,

68
00:03:59,955 --> 00:04:02,380
which knocks out power transmission.

69
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:05,747
But the power plant itself and the generating equipment

70
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:06,792
are not destroyed.

71
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:10,618
You can see — isn't that remarkable?

72
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:14,458
Actually these warfare technologies —

73
00:04:14,458 --> 00:04:16,780
take U.S. drones, for example.

74
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:19,725
The Lucas drone.

75
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:23,289
Then there are unmanned speedboats, unmanned vessels.

76
00:04:23,289 --> 00:04:26,540
In the lower-left corner you can see one called Starshield.

77
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:29,470
That's also from SpaceX.

78
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,406
Starlink is for civilian use.

79
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:35,024
Starshield is for military use.

80
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:39,016
So if you take apart the nose cones of those drones,

81
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:41,021
you'll find receivers inside,

82
00:04:41,021 --> 00:04:44,400
and those tap into Starshield for navigation.

83
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,200
And in the upper-right corner,

84
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:48,754
that one is a laser cannon.

85
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:55,576
Actually, since around 2023,

86
00:04:55,576 --> 00:05:00,510
one thing that's become very popular is the so-called saturation attack.

87
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:03,755
You use cheap weapons — drones, for instance,

88
00:05:03,755 --> 00:05:06,019
or some inexpensive missiles.

89
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:09,065
And here's the thing:

90
00:05:09,065 --> 00:05:13,593
the missiles used to shoot them down are far more expensive.

91
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,868
You've probably heard the 1-to-100 ratio —

92
00:05:16,868 --> 00:05:19,845
meaning the value of one interceptor missile

93
00:05:19,845 --> 00:05:22,000
is 100 times that of one drone.

94
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,728
So while the enemy is spending pennies,

95
00:05:24,728 --> 00:05:26,238
you're getting drained dry.

96
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:28,097
So you can see —

97
00:05:28,097 --> 00:05:31,812
in this conflict, you're seeing a lot of very cheap weapons.

98
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:36,261
So from all of these weapons here,

99
00:05:36,261 --> 00:05:38,590
from every single one of them,

100
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:42,085
you can really see that the technology behind

101
00:05:42,085 --> 00:05:45,580
them is quite advanced — remarkably advanced.

102
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:49,411
Someone once said: every weapon humans have invented

103
00:05:49,411 --> 00:05:52,952
has eventually been deployed on a battlefield.

104
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:56,723
And in fact, throughout the history of war,

105
00:05:56,723 --> 00:05:59,348
weapons have gone through several —

106
00:05:59,348 --> 00:06:02,520
three major revolutions, you could say.

107
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:05,829
Let me walk through them as a kind of framework.

108
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:09,132
If someone asked you, in all of history,

109
00:06:09,132 --> 00:06:11,106
which was the greatest empire?

110
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:16,464
You might say the Mongol Empire — and you'd be right.

111
00:06:16,464 --> 00:06:19,053
Because across the vast expanse of Eurasia,

112
00:06:19,053 --> 00:06:23,320
the Mongol Empire stretched across an enormous swath of the continent.

113
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:25,526
Why were they so formidable?

114
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:29,781
The Mongols' greatest strength was their cavalry.

115
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:33,395
I think in this painting —

116
00:06:33,395 --> 00:06:34,884
when I found this image,

117
00:06:34,884 --> 00:06:36,746
I felt that the person who painted it

118
00:06:36,746 --> 00:06:38,840
really understood the Mongol cavalry.

119
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:39,304
Why?

120
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,020
Because one of the Mongols' deadliest assets was their bow —

121
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,935
the famous recurve bow.

122
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:49,274
See how both ends curve outward?

123
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:52,904
Compared with bows of the 13th century,

124
00:06:52,904 --> 00:06:55,640
it was three or four times more powerful.

125
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:58,542
At close range it could pierce armor.

126
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,250
The other thing was the stirrup.

127
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,555
The foothold you step into when mounting — the stirrup.

128
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,500
The Mongols didn't invent the stirrup,

129
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:10,938
but they pushed its use to the limit.

130
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:16,506
Why is that? Well, because of the stirrup,

131
00:07:16,506 --> 00:07:19,008
the rider could actually stand up on his

132
00:07:19,008 --> 00:07:21,884
horse's back, and from there shoot arrows in a

133
00:07:21,884 --> 00:07:23,948
full 360 degrees, all around him,

134
00:07:23,948 --> 00:07:26,699
pinning his opponent down. And combined with

135
00:07:26,699 --> 00:07:28,513
that incredibly powerful bow,

136
00:07:28,513 --> 00:07:31,264
they had a famous tactic called the Parthian

137
00:07:31,264 --> 00:07:34,203
shot — so don't ever assume they're beaten just

138
00:07:34,203 --> 00:07:37,142
because they turn and flee and go chasing after

139
00:07:37,142 --> 00:07:40,019
them, because they'll spin right around in the

140
00:07:40,019 --> 00:07:42,520
saddle and put an arrow straight in you.

141
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:44,815
That's why in the 13th century

142
00:07:44,815 --> 00:07:49,560
the Mongols swept across everything — no one could stand against them.

143
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:52,695
So what was the first weapons revolution?

144
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:01,438
It was gunpowder — applying gunpowder to military use.

145
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,613
That gave us firearms, and also artillery.

146
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:13,327
So if someone asks you which was the largest empire,

147
00:08:13,327 --> 00:08:16,462
by sheer area, it wasn't the Mongols.

148
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,798
By area — and you know this one well —

149
00:08:19,798 --> 00:08:23,680
it was the empire on which the sun never set: the British Empire.

150
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:24,551
In history,

151
00:08:24,551 --> 00:08:29,021
by total area, the British Empire was the largest.

152
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:31,825
That was the first revolution

153
00:08:31,825 --> 00:08:33,915
in warfare and in weapons.

154
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:42,028
If someone says to you "rare, medium-rare,"

155
00:08:42,028 --> 00:08:44,989
or "well done" — what comes to mind?

156
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:50,640
You'd probably think someone's teaching you how to grill steak.

157
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,194
But actually, this is military jargon —

158
00:08:53,194 --> 00:08:54,820
a technical term.

159
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:59,060
It refers to one major country — namely, the United States.

160
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:02,311
If the U.S. ever came under nuclear attack,

161
00:09:02,311 --> 00:09:05,271
these are the response options on the table.

162
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:08,246
Rare, medium-rare, or well done?

163
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:13,880
What they're really talking about isn't steak — it's the Earth.

164
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:18,424
So if you're hit by a nuclear strike,

165
00:09:18,424 --> 00:09:21,849
what's the response? Mutually assured destruction?

166
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,316
Or some other kind of reply?

167
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:30,578
So the second revolution in weapons history

168
00:09:30,578 --> 00:09:33,423
was the invention of the nuclear bomb.

169
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:39,788
After the nuclear bomb was invented,

170
00:09:39,788 --> 00:09:43,097
it wasn't long before two were dropped on Japan.

171
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:48,580
And America has remained extremely powerful — even today.

172
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:52,063
Nuclear weapons have two distinctive features.

173
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:56,966
First, you need nuclear fuel.

174
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:02,520
And that nuclear fuel is fundamentally hard to obtain.

175
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:07,338
For instance, Iran today desperately wants to become a nuclear power.

176
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:11,762
But refining and obtaining that fuel is not easy.

177
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:18,260
On top of that, there's the international Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty.

178
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:20,930
So from 1945 to today,

179
00:10:20,930 --> 00:10:23,949
only ten countries possess nuclear weapons —

180
00:10:23,949 --> 00:10:26,561
that is, nuclear capability.

181
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:31,410
One thing to note: the last two added —

182
00:10:31,410 --> 00:10:35,706
I've marked their dates here. I don't know if you can see.

183
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:38,790
The last two countries to join the nuclear club —

184
00:10:38,790 --> 00:10:42,700
the latest one was North Korea, in 2006.

185
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:45,428
And the one before that was Pakistan.

186
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:49,158
Today's gathering has a theme,

187
00:10:49,158 --> 00:10:50,957
and it's called "machine killers,"

188
00:10:50,957 --> 00:10:52,815
or "killer robots."

189
00:10:55,020 --> 00:10:56,878
Robots in everyday life

190
00:10:56,878 --> 00:11:00,244
are already affecting everyone in many ways.

191
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:05,234
For example, here, in the upper-left, that's Waymo.

192
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:07,118
Have any of you ridden in one over there?

193
00:11:07,118 --> 00:11:09,846
In LA downtown, for example,

194
00:11:09,846 --> 00:11:11,529
you'll see them soon enough —

195
00:11:11,529 --> 00:11:13,793
those self-driving taxis.

196
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:16,190
Waymo is easy to recognize:

197
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:18,686
sensors all over the roof and sides.

198
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:21,234
And the one right next to it —

199
00:11:21,234 --> 00:11:23,969
that one's like the ones they use at Shake

200
00:11:23,969 --> 00:11:26,705
Shack — those little robots that go around

201
00:11:26,705 --> 00:11:29,505
delivering documents and delivering food to

202
00:11:29,505 --> 00:11:29,961
people.

203
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:33,781
Some robots — for example, the Optimus,

204
00:11:33,781 --> 00:11:35,987
which is Tesla's.

205
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:40,414
There's been recent news in the U.S.:

206
00:11:40,414 --> 00:11:42,736
they've started putting it behind the counter.

207
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:46,907
For instance, there's a place in LA called Diamond Bar —

208
00:11:46,907 --> 00:11:49,520
their City Hall has one of these robots.

209
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:51,243
You walk up and it asks you

210
00:11:51,243 --> 00:11:52,927
what it can help you with.

211
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:56,525
Robots are even helping with law enforcement.

212
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:01,632
A few days ago I saw this vehicle.

213
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:06,381
I don't know if you've been following this technology —

214
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:08,000
it's called the Cybercab.

215
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,090
Now, Waymo belongs to Google,

216
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:12,586
and the Cybercab belongs to Tesla.

217
00:12:15,300 --> 00:12:17,390
So I have to ask a question:

218
00:12:17,390 --> 00:12:19,305
if there's nothing special about it,

219
00:12:19,305 --> 00:12:21,047
it's just a self-driving taxi,

220
00:12:21,047 --> 00:12:23,543
then what's so special about the Cybercab?

221
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:26,711
Have you noticed?

222
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:29,665
Well, I actually saw one of them being

223
00:12:29,665 --> 00:12:31,953
test-driven out on the street —

224
00:12:31,953 --> 00:12:34,831
and this is the special thing about it:

225
00:12:34,831 --> 00:12:37,193
it has no steering wheel at all.

226
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,800
And no pedals either.

227
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:42,656
If you think of the Dragon capsule —

228
00:12:42,656 --> 00:12:46,926
SpaceX's spacecraft that takes people to the International Space Station —

229
00:12:46,926 --> 00:12:48,300
it's basically the same idea.

230
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:50,680
You get on board and there's just a touchscreen.

231
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:52,858
Just a touchscreen in there.

232
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:55,209
No steering wheel at all.

233
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:59,654
So if something goes wrong, you wouldn't know what to do.

234
00:13:00,200 --> 00:13:03,044
Today we're talking about killer robots —

235
00:13:03,044 --> 00:13:04,205
"Killer Robot."

236
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,200
That's actually the colloquial term.

237
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:09,058
What's the technical name?

238
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:13,025
It's "Lethal Autonomous Weapon" (LAW).

239
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:18,045
Lethal — and autonomous — weapons.

240
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:20,944
As the name suggests, it's a weapon,

241
00:13:20,944 --> 00:13:22,685
but it has two key elements.

242
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:26,299
First, it's equipped with lethal weaponry.

243
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,040
And the second element?

244
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:32,581
It's autonomous — we'll get to that in a moment.

245
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:34,529
So the killer robot

246
00:13:34,529 --> 00:13:37,142
is defined by these two traits:

247
00:13:37,142 --> 00:13:39,638
first, it carries lethal weapons,

248
00:13:39,638 --> 00:13:42,540
and second, it acts autonomously.

249
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:45,727
Now in your mind,

250
00:13:45,727 --> 00:13:48,165
what does a killer robot look like?

251
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:51,061
Something like this?

252
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:57,868
A while back, there were movies —

253
00:13:57,868 --> 00:14:02,222
Terminator, or Robocop — you've seen the type.

254
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:05,454
But those are all fiction.

255
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:08,895
Something closer to reality would be,

256
00:14:08,895 --> 00:14:11,660
for example, Big Dog — the one with a gun

257
00:14:11,660 --> 00:14:14,628
mounted on top of it — or else some of those

258
00:14:14,628 --> 00:14:17,460
robots that run along on tank-like tracks.

259
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:19,143
But you might say — no, no.

260
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:21,666
The robots I saw on TV —

261
00:14:21,666 --> 00:14:25,497
those killer robots aren't like that. They look like this.

262
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:31,494
At the Spring Festival Gala recently, I saw these killer robots —

263
00:14:31,494 --> 00:14:33,352
ones that can throw punches.

264
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:39,960
Actually, humanoid robots may not be the most advantageous form.

265
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:43,144
Because two legs are never going to outrun four legs.

266
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,765
And if the terrain is rough,

267
00:14:46,765 --> 00:14:51,177
I don't think they'd be as stable as tracked vehicles either.

268
00:14:55,180 --> 00:14:56,564
Besides the Iran war,

269
00:14:56,564 --> 00:15:01,620
there's another war — going on in Eastern Europe — that's been raging for over four years now.

270
00:15:01,620 --> 00:15:04,232
That's the Russia-Ukraine war.

271
00:15:04,260 --> 00:15:07,145
Last year, there was actually a scene just like

272
00:15:07,145 --> 00:15:09,601
this that played out: you had a group of

273
00:15:09,601 --> 00:15:12,056
Russian soldiers, and they surrendered —

274
00:15:12,056 --> 00:15:14,695
they gave themselves up to one of the enemy

275
00:15:14,695 --> 00:15:17,212
units. But that unit didn't have a single

276
00:15:17,212 --> 00:15:18,440
living person in it.

277
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:19,833
They were all robots.

278
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:22,123
I don't know if you saw the news.

279
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:24,459
I saw it on YouTube.

280
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:27,040
I'm not sure if you can still find it.

281
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:29,304
Here's what happened: a drone —

282
00:15:29,304 --> 00:15:31,103
a Ukrainian drone —

283
00:15:31,103 --> 00:15:32,671
flew over a trench

284
00:15:32,671 --> 00:15:35,341
and spotted some Russian soldiers.

285
00:15:37,860 --> 00:15:40,066
Some of them fired at the drone.

286
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:46,029
Not long after, a vehicle showed up —

287
00:15:46,029 --> 00:15:49,454
you can see it on the bottom right of the screen.

288
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:51,557
What was that vehicle?

289
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:53,694
It was actually a bomb.

290
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:57,356
A four-wheeled bomb — it drove right up to the trench,

291
00:15:57,356 --> 00:15:59,360
intending to wipe them all out at once.

292
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:03,577
But the Russian soldiers saw it and raised a white flag —

293
00:16:03,577 --> 00:16:05,120
signaling surrender.

294
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:08,313
Then another vehicle, equipped with a machine gun, came up,

295
00:16:08,313 --> 00:16:09,474
disarmed them,

296
00:16:09,474 --> 00:16:12,492
and escorted them back to a POW camp.

297
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:18,378
Can you believe that's even possible?

298
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:20,801
If you've been following the news,

299
00:16:20,801 --> 00:16:23,240
you know that in these four years,

300
00:16:23,240 --> 00:16:26,084
Ukraine has become a military powerhouse,

301
00:16:26,084 --> 00:16:28,580
especially in unmanned systems:

302
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:36,359
unmanned aircraft, unmanned ground vehicles, unmanned combat vehicles, unmanned naval vessels.

303
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:40,760
The unmanned vessels are particularly famous. Why?

304
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:43,895
Because they've kept the Black Sea Fleet in check —

305
00:16:43,895 --> 00:16:45,810
Russia's Black Sea Fleet.

306
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:50,086
When controlling these unmanned machines,

307
00:16:50,086 --> 00:16:53,511
they usually use what's called "First Person View" —

308
00:16:53,511 --> 00:16:55,775
that is, first-person vision.

309
00:16:59,020 --> 00:17:00,239
What the aircraft sees,

310
00:17:00,239 --> 00:17:02,967
what the camera sees, is what you see.

311
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:06,860
Like in the image you're looking at:

312
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:08,891
the operator, the controller —

313
00:17:08,891 --> 00:17:10,981
what he sees is exactly what

314
00:17:10,981 --> 00:17:13,129
the drone's camera is capturing.

315
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:17,336
Starting from late 2024,

316
00:17:17,336 --> 00:17:18,671
on the Ukrainian battlefield,

317
00:17:18,671 --> 00:17:22,967
they began implementing what's called coordinated combat.

318
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:29,219
A single soldier, a single individual,

319
00:17:29,219 --> 00:17:31,947
doesn't just control the ground vehicles —

320
00:17:31,947 --> 00:17:35,952
he can also simultaneously control aerial drones.

321
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:40,709
So there's surveillance — like the earlier example:

322
00:17:40,709 --> 00:17:43,031
he spots the Russian soldiers,

323
00:17:43,031 --> 00:17:45,295
then can dispatch a bomb-carrying vehicle

324
00:17:45,295 --> 00:17:47,037
to take out the enemy.

325
00:17:47,580 --> 00:17:50,308
You can see this is coordinated combat —

326
00:17:50,308 --> 00:17:52,456
letting a single soldier control

327
00:17:52,456 --> 00:17:56,636
unmanned weapons across sea, land, and air.

328
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:01,714
On top of that, over these few years, artificial intelligence

329
00:18:01,714 --> 00:18:03,629
has been developing on the battlefield,

330
00:18:03,629 --> 00:18:06,938
making the effectiveness even more formidable —

331
00:18:06,938 --> 00:18:10,595
the effectiveness of using robots. Why?

332
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:16,800
Because AI can take massive amounts of sensor data —

333
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:22,140
huge volumes of data — and digest and analyze it,

334
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:24,462
assess the battlefield situation,

335
00:18:24,462 --> 00:18:27,016
and then offer options for action.

336
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:30,748
So have you noticed in recent months,

337
00:18:30,748 --> 00:18:33,040
the U.S. military looks pretty impressive?

338
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:37,212
For example, the capture of Maduro — done in a few hours overnight,

339
00:18:37,212 --> 00:18:40,774
and they got him. Now in Central and South America,

340
00:18:40,774 --> 00:18:43,540
Venezuela counts as a regional military power.

341
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:45,660
But just like that, the capture succeeded.

342
00:18:45,660 --> 00:18:48,446
I'll give you more examples and you'll understand.

343
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:52,602
I wonder if any of you have seen this movie —

344
00:18:52,602 --> 00:18:55,679
it's called Zero Dark Thirty.

345
00:18:55,800 --> 00:18:57,483
Some people mistakenly think

346
00:18:57,483 --> 00:18:59,515
that if you want to watch war movies,

347
00:18:59,515 --> 00:19:02,011
this is one of the top ten.

348
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:05,222
What does "Zero Dark Thirty" mean?

349
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:09,693
The Chinese title I think you already know.

350
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,005
Usually for military operations,

351
00:19:14,005 --> 00:19:17,500
they pick the hours between 12 a.m. and 3 a.m.

352
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:19,731
Because the body's circadian rhythm tells us

353
00:19:19,731 --> 00:19:22,220
that most people between midnight and 3 a.m.

354
00:19:22,220 --> 00:19:25,420
are at their most vulnerable, their sleepiest.

355
00:19:25,420 --> 00:19:28,148
So "Zero-Thirty" means 12:30 —

356
00:19:28,148 --> 00:19:30,993
an operation at 30 past midnight.

357
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:37,996
There's one scene in it that goes something

358
00:19:37,996 --> 00:19:40,825
like this: this female CIA analyst,

359
00:19:40,825 --> 00:19:44,381
this woman, manages to track down bin Laden,

360
00:19:44,381 --> 00:19:47,291
who had been hiding out in Pakistan.

361
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:51,035
But her superiors keep delaying action.

362
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:52,520
No action. Why?

363
00:19:52,520 --> 00:19:54,720
Because they're afraid of a diplomatic incident.

364
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:57,274
She keeps asking: how certain are we

365
00:19:57,274 --> 00:20:00,351
that bin Laden is there? In that compound?

366
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:04,276
This scene: the CIA analyst

367
00:20:04,276 --> 00:20:07,817
goes to her boss's door every day and writes a number.

368
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:10,780
Like today: she writes "21 days."

369
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:13,122
It's been 21 days since they found the compound,

370
00:20:13,122 --> 00:20:14,334
and still no action.

371
00:20:14,334 --> 00:20:16,515
Until what — 159 days.

372
00:20:16,515 --> 00:20:19,359
Over four months with no action.

373
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:22,541
Finally, the operation went ahead.

374
00:20:24,540 --> 00:20:25,817
In this Iran war,

375
00:20:25,817 --> 00:20:29,648
the most striking thing — was the first day.

376
00:20:32,780 --> 00:20:35,160
On day one, they took out Khamenei.

377
00:20:38,500 --> 00:20:41,444
And it wasn't just him — on that very first

378
00:20:41,444 --> 00:20:44,388
day, they had already taken out 48 of their

379
00:20:44,388 --> 00:20:46,442
most senior military generals,

380
00:20:46,442 --> 00:20:49,660
along with a number of other important leaders.

381
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:51,880
Remarkable. Truly remarkable.

382
00:20:51,880 --> 00:20:53,738
How could they accomplish this?

383
00:20:54,900 --> 00:20:57,164
Beyond the precision of the weapons —

384
00:20:57,164 --> 00:20:58,673
look at the upper-left:

385
00:20:58,673 --> 00:21:02,795
Khamenei's residence — a precision strike, right?

386
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:05,360
The building collapsed.

387
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:07,276
And several deep holes,

388
00:21:07,276 --> 00:21:10,410
because they used bunker-buster munitions.

389
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:15,277
Meanwhile the surrounding buildings were not destroyed.

390
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:19,438
So what's actually happening here?

391
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:23,146
They use extensive surveillance —

392
00:21:23,146 --> 00:21:25,120
street cameras, for example,

393
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:29,357
massive surveillance, and AI to analyze it all.

394
00:21:29,660 --> 00:21:33,375
They confirm all the targeted individuals are on site,

395
00:21:33,375 --> 00:21:34,943
then decide when —

396
00:21:34,943 --> 00:21:36,742
whether to strike at that moment.

397
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:41,737
And when they do strike — what weapon?

398
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:44,905
You may have heard of one called

399
00:21:44,905 --> 00:21:47,924
"Blue Spear" — Blue Sparrow.

400
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:53,596
This weapon is very unusual. Why?

401
00:21:53,940 --> 00:21:57,260
A normal missile, once launched, flies forward.

402
00:21:57,260 --> 00:21:58,860
But this one flies upward.

403
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:01,382
It punches out to the edge of the atmosphere,

404
00:22:01,382 --> 00:22:03,260
the edge of space.

405
00:22:03,260 --> 00:22:05,001
Why does it do that?

406
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:08,273
First, there's less drag at the edge of space —

407
00:22:08,273 --> 00:22:10,600
so it has much longer range.

408
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:13,386
When it comes back down to ground level,

409
00:22:13,386 --> 00:22:15,592
re-entering the atmosphere,

410
00:22:15,592 --> 00:22:17,450
it dives so fast

411
00:22:17,450 --> 00:22:20,410
that interception becomes very difficult.

412
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:23,719
Understand?

413
00:22:23,720 --> 00:22:25,287
There's actually another reason.

414
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:28,873
Because look — this is day one of the war,

415
00:22:28,873 --> 00:22:30,760
the early stage of the conflict.

416
00:22:30,760 --> 00:22:33,826
You don't yet know how much of Iran's air defense

417
00:22:33,826 --> 00:22:35,800
has been suppressed or destroyed.

418
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:39,283
So they chose this weapon for exactly that reason.

419
00:22:40,680 --> 00:22:44,294
You know, radar — no matter how it looks from the outside —

420
00:22:44,294 --> 00:22:48,360
inside it has an antenna that rotates 360 degrees.

421
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:50,566
The principle is that simple:

422
00:22:50,566 --> 00:22:52,598
you emit signals outward,

423
00:22:52,598 --> 00:22:54,978
they bounce off something and come back,

424
00:22:54,978 --> 00:22:56,835
and now you know what's out there.

425
00:22:58,240 --> 00:23:00,017
It scans 360 degrees, right?

426
00:23:00,017 --> 00:23:02,455
But most radars have a blind spot.

427
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:06,427
That blind spot is the zenith —

428
00:23:06,427 --> 00:23:07,980
directly overhead.

429
00:23:07,980 --> 00:23:11,115
You rarely see a radar sweeping straight up.

430
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:12,985
Understand?

431
00:23:13,020 --> 00:23:16,387
So this Blue Sparrow's special feature is:

432
00:23:16,387 --> 00:23:18,767
it comes in right at the zenith —

433
00:23:18,767 --> 00:23:20,683
straight down on top of you.

434
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:23,341
So why on day one?

435
00:23:25,320 --> 00:23:27,120
What I want to show you is:

436
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:29,906
all that intelligence — the analysis of it,

437
00:23:29,906 --> 00:23:33,331
the timing, the weapon operations —

438
00:23:33,331 --> 00:23:37,569
all of it was filtered through AI to inform the decision.

439
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:41,600
The result is obvious.

440
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:48,671
Alright — so in the face of all these unmanned

441
00:23:48,671 --> 00:23:51,519
weapons, all these drones, there are actually

442
00:23:51,519 --> 00:23:54,241
counter-measures too, ways that people have

443
00:23:54,241 --> 00:23:55,760
found to deal with them.

444
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:57,850
Can you see what this image shows?

445
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:02,074
Look at this city —

446
00:24:02,074 --> 00:24:05,266
doesn't it look like there's a spider web over it?

447
00:24:07,520 --> 00:24:09,784
This is a town in eastern Ukraine.

448
00:24:13,660 --> 00:24:14,879
What are those?

449
00:24:17,380 --> 00:24:20,196
You see, when one of these drones is sent up

450
00:24:20,196 --> 00:24:23,204
into the sky, the way it works is that it keeps

451
00:24:23,204 --> 00:24:25,635
in constant contact with its operator,

452
00:24:25,635 --> 00:24:28,003
and it does that through radio waves.

453
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:32,133
How do you counter it?

454
00:24:32,740 --> 00:24:34,481
You send a jamming signal —

455
00:24:34,481 --> 00:24:36,803
an electronic jamming system.

456
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:41,497
Once contact is lost,

457
00:24:41,497 --> 00:24:43,122
you can't control it anymore.

458
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:46,190
Understand? So how do you solve that problem?

459
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:50,325
How do you solve it? They use fiber optics —

460
00:24:50,325 --> 00:24:52,067
fiber-optic cables —

461
00:24:52,067 --> 00:24:53,634
to tether the drone.

462
00:24:56,720 --> 00:24:59,739
That way it's not affected by radio jamming.

463
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:04,017
And through the fiber, you can transmit the same signals:

464
00:25:04,017 --> 00:25:05,643
first, control of the drone;

465
00:25:05,643 --> 00:25:07,036
second, the video feed.

466
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:10,942
You can still operate it from a first-person view.

467
00:25:11,960 --> 00:25:13,353
Do you see what I mean?

468
00:25:13,420 --> 00:25:18,020
And those fiber cables are very long — tens of kilometers.

469
00:25:18,020 --> 00:25:19,716
Each one is tens of kilometers.

470
00:25:19,716 --> 00:25:21,368
After the target is destroyed,

471
00:25:21,368 --> 00:25:24,500
the fiber stays behind on the battlefield.

472
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:28,447
Over time, the whole battlefield looks like a spider web.

473
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:31,848
That's what those fibers are.

474
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,657
If you know fiber optics — it doesn't break easily.

475
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:40,900
But as you can imagine, there are real problems with it.

476
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:44,963
Beyond all that fiber being left behind on the battlefield,

477
00:25:44,963 --> 00:25:50,246
there's the fact that no matter how long it is, it's only tens of kilometers.

478
00:25:52,420 --> 00:25:54,858
So how do you solve that?

479
00:25:56,620 --> 00:25:59,581
How do you really solve the jamming problem?

480
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:04,399
A few weeks ago, in

481
00:26:04,399 --> 00:26:07,418
Foreign Affairs magazine, there was an article.

482
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:14,658
And the man who wrote it is actually quite well

483
00:26:14,658 --> 00:26:18,041
known — this is someone who was a four-star

484
00:26:18,041 --> 00:26:21,345
general, and who also served as the former

485
00:26:21,345 --> 00:26:22,918
Director of the CIA.

486
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:28,085
He wrote an article about the "autonomous battlefield."

487
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:31,566
And what does he mean by that? Well,

488
00:26:31,566 --> 00:26:33,936
here's what he said: in the end,

489
00:26:33,936 --> 00:26:37,121
whether or not you win a war doesn't really

490
00:26:37,121 --> 00:26:40,528
come down to how many drones a country happens

491
00:26:40,528 --> 00:26:43,935
to have, or how many unmanned weapons it's got

492
00:26:43,935 --> 00:26:47,046
— what it truly comes down to is how those

493
00:26:47,046 --> 00:26:49,860
unmanned weapons make their decisions.

494
00:26:49,860 --> 00:26:52,066
If, when you've detected the enemy,

495
00:26:52,066 --> 00:26:56,071
before firing, you still need a human — a soldier —

496
00:26:56,071 --> 00:26:58,219
to press the firing button,

497
00:26:58,219 --> 00:27:03,153
that operation, that reaction, is already too slow.

498
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:06,536
Do you understand what I mean?

499
00:27:06,620 --> 00:27:08,768
So look at Ukraine today —

500
00:27:08,768 --> 00:27:12,599
the Interceptor on the left was developed by Ukraine,

501
00:27:12,599 --> 00:27:17,533
which is now helping Gulf states deal with Iran's drones.

502
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:22,255
On the right are drones invented by the U.S.

503
00:27:24,620 --> 00:27:26,620
What's special about these drones?

504
00:27:26,620 --> 00:27:28,478
First, they're AI-guided.

505
00:27:29,220 --> 00:27:30,729
Their pilot is AI.

506
00:27:32,220 --> 00:27:33,729
That is, when you launch one,

507
00:27:33,729 --> 00:27:35,645
you've given it a mission,

508
00:27:35,645 --> 00:27:37,793
and it will carry out that mission.

509
00:27:40,860 --> 00:27:42,311
When you send it into the air,

510
00:27:42,311 --> 00:27:43,762
what do they expect?

511
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:48,240
That contact will be lost quickly — because of jamming.

512
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:50,549
But because you've given it an order,

513
00:27:50,549 --> 00:27:53,120
it will execute. How?

514
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:55,732
It will autonomously seek out the target

515
00:27:55,732 --> 00:27:57,242
and destroy it.

516
00:27:59,200 --> 00:28:02,160
After that, several scenarios are possible.

517
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:03,669
First, what?

518
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:09,453
The target it was looking for has already been destroyed —

519
00:28:09,453 --> 00:28:11,543
then it will safely return.

520
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:12,780
Or what?

521
00:28:12,780 --> 00:28:17,424
It finds another target. Once its target is destroyed,

522
00:28:17,424 --> 00:28:19,456
it finds another target on its own,

523
00:28:19,456 --> 00:28:21,720
finds another target, and then destroys it.

524
00:28:23,160 --> 00:28:24,205
Or what?

525
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:26,530
Or you re-establish contact with the drone

526
00:28:26,530 --> 00:28:30,593
and change its orders, or give it a different command.

527
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:33,780
Do you understand what I mean?

528
00:28:33,780 --> 00:28:36,102
Simply put, once you've put it in the air,

529
00:28:36,102 --> 00:28:38,656
it executes the order on its own.

530
00:28:40,720 --> 00:28:42,984
At the moment of dropping the bomb,

531
00:28:42,984 --> 00:28:45,364
you don't have to press anything again.

532
00:28:49,220 --> 00:28:52,529
It carries out this kind of order by itself.

533
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:55,131
Are you following?

534
00:28:56,180 --> 00:28:59,315
I think this is exactly where the problem lies.

535
00:29:02,460 --> 00:29:05,990
Because traditionally, with so-called killer robots,

536
00:29:05,990 --> 00:29:07,900
there has always been a red line.

537
00:29:07,900 --> 00:29:09,061
What is that red line?

538
00:29:09,540 --> 00:29:13,894
At the final moment, when the decision to fire is made,

539
00:29:13,894 --> 00:29:15,577
where does the control sit?

540
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:19,456
Is it with the soldier operating it?

541
00:29:19,740 --> 00:29:22,178
Or do you hand it over to AI to decide?

542
00:29:23,700 --> 00:29:26,661
That's the red line. That's the trigger.

543
00:29:27,800 --> 00:29:29,193
And what did we just see?

544
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:32,100
Because of how the battlefield has evolved,

545
00:29:32,100 --> 00:29:36,280
they've developed these drones, these unmanned systems,

546
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:38,544
and to deal with jamming signals,

547
00:29:38,544 --> 00:29:40,343
they made this arrangement:

548
00:29:40,343 --> 00:29:44,058
once you launch it, it executes the mission on its own.

549
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:48,780
AI is its navigator. Understand?

550
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:51,221
Some people will ask:

551
00:29:51,221 --> 00:29:54,704
how is that even acceptable? Once it's up there,

552
00:29:54,704 --> 00:29:58,013
it finds its own targets and opens fire on its own.

553
00:29:58,660 --> 00:30:00,518
Does it know who it's targeting?

554
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:03,914
Does it know whether it's right or wrong?

555
00:30:04,220 --> 00:30:05,780
Is it acceptable?

556
00:30:05,780 --> 00:30:07,521
But you have to understand —

557
00:30:07,521 --> 00:30:11,121
in four years of war between Russia and Ukraine,

558
00:30:11,121 --> 00:30:13,036
what does Ukraine want most?

559
00:30:14,100 --> 00:30:15,145
To win the war.

560
00:30:15,700 --> 00:30:16,875
Whether it's moral,

561
00:30:16,875 --> 00:30:21,240
whether it's appropriate — leave that to the academics to debate.

562
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:24,259
Understand? So little by little,

563
00:30:24,259 --> 00:30:26,290
that red line has been crossed.

564
00:30:28,160 --> 00:30:31,013
And that has produced what we just talked about —

565
00:30:31,013 --> 00:30:33,760
the killer robot's two defining traits.

566
00:30:33,760 --> 00:30:37,359
First: equipped with lethal weapons —

567
00:30:37,359 --> 00:30:39,333
guns, bombs, whatever.

568
00:30:41,160 --> 00:30:44,875
Second: capable of autonomous execution.

569
00:30:47,040 --> 00:30:50,523
And the boundary keeps getting blurrier and blurrier.

570
00:30:51,240 --> 00:30:53,388
Getting blurrier — if you follow the news,

571
00:30:53,388 --> 00:30:56,000
recently there's been an AI company

572
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,903
in a lawsuit with the U.S. Department of Defense.

573
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:02,578
Because they're very firm —

574
00:31:02,578 --> 00:31:05,074
they believe that AI on the battlefield

575
00:31:05,074 --> 00:31:07,976
can only be allowed to go so far.

576
00:31:11,160 --> 00:31:14,269
They think it shouldn't be allowed to act autonomously.

577
00:31:14,269 --> 00:31:16,698
The Defense Department, of course, says:

578
00:31:16,698 --> 00:31:18,900
we can't work with a company like that.

579
00:31:18,900 --> 00:31:20,660
So they canceled the contract.

580
00:31:20,660 --> 00:31:21,879
And now it's in court.

581
00:31:24,560 --> 00:31:25,779
So what can you see?

582
00:31:26,300 --> 00:31:27,925
This is the trend.

583
00:31:28,080 --> 00:31:30,170
And I believe this represents

584
00:31:30,170 --> 00:31:33,304
the coming third revolution in weapons of war.

585
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:36,158
What makes it different from nuclear weapons?

586
00:31:37,640 --> 00:31:39,730
The materials are very easy to obtain.

587
00:31:43,180 --> 00:31:45,096
You can even use 3D printers

588
00:31:45,096 --> 00:31:46,663
to print these weapons.

589
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:50,621
You may have heard a recent news story —

590
00:31:50,621 --> 00:31:53,523
Beijing banning certain people, banning drone flights,

591
00:31:53,523 --> 00:31:56,716
banning drone modifications. It's very simple:

592
00:31:56,716 --> 00:32:00,837
all you need is to build a drone and strap explosives to it,

593
00:32:00,837 --> 00:32:04,320
and you've got a decapitation strike capability.

594
00:32:06,780 --> 00:32:10,026
So this is really quite different from nuclear

595
00:32:10,026 --> 00:32:13,272
weapons — because here, the materials you need

596
00:32:13,272 --> 00:32:16,588
are far more common, far more widely available,

597
00:32:16,588 --> 00:32:19,340
and much, much easier to spread around.

598
00:32:19,340 --> 00:32:21,858
And take Ukraine today — in fact,

599
00:32:21,858 --> 00:32:24,681
if you just go and look it up online,

600
00:32:24,681 --> 00:32:27,428
you'll find that they're now able to

601
00:32:27,428 --> 00:32:30,099
manufacture drones by the millions,

602
00:32:30,099 --> 00:32:31,472
every single year.

603
00:32:32,140 --> 00:32:35,780
At the start of the war they had almost no military industry.

604
00:32:35,780 --> 00:32:38,686
And now, at this point, they can turn out

605
00:32:38,686 --> 00:32:41,663
drones numbering in the millions in just a

606
00:32:41,663 --> 00:32:42,514
single year.

607
00:32:42,980 --> 00:32:45,070
Alright — you might ask:

608
00:32:45,070 --> 00:32:48,901
these weapons revolutions, the development of these weapons —

609
00:32:48,901 --> 00:32:51,223
what does it have to do with us?

610
00:32:54,880 --> 00:32:57,084
In the Book of Revelation, in the Bible,

611
00:32:57,084 --> 00:32:59,900
there are descriptions of future disasters.

612
00:32:59,900 --> 00:33:02,977
While we were singing earlier, I had a thought —

613
00:33:02,977 --> 00:33:06,053
because the Lord has saved us, and He will save us still.

614
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:10,178
He saved us from sin,

615
00:33:10,178 --> 00:33:12,790
and He will save us from disaster as well.

616
00:33:13,480 --> 00:33:15,744
Speaking of future disasters,

617
00:33:15,744 --> 00:33:19,517
one of the things mentioned is the massive loss of life.

618
00:33:20,580 --> 00:33:23,424
This refers especially to two moments.

619
00:33:26,940 --> 00:33:30,365
The first is the opening of the seals,

620
00:33:30,365 --> 00:33:32,919
the opening of the early seals.

621
00:33:33,480 --> 00:33:35,047
As it says here:

622
00:33:35,047 --> 00:33:37,311
when the fourth seal was opened,

623
00:33:37,311 --> 00:33:39,633
I heard the fourth living creature say,

624
00:33:39,633 --> 00:33:42,071
"Come." And I looked,

625
00:33:42,071 --> 00:33:47,934
and behold, a pale horse, and the name of the one riding it was Death.

626
00:33:49,600 --> 00:33:53,000
Hades followed with him. Authority was given to them

627
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:57,716
to kill with sword, famine, plague, and wild beasts —

628
00:33:57,716 --> 00:34:01,500
killing a quarter of the people of the earth.

629
00:34:01,500 --> 00:34:03,492
Before that there were three horses:

630
00:34:03,492 --> 00:34:06,180
a white horse, a red horse, and a black horse.

631
00:34:06,180 --> 00:34:08,784
That's actually an echo of what came earlier —

632
00:34:08,784 --> 00:34:11,880
the red horse representing the sword, that is, war.

633
00:34:11,880 --> 00:34:15,537
The black horse — what does it represent? Famine.

634
00:34:17,280 --> 00:34:20,182
And there's plague, too. I wonder —

635
00:34:20,182 --> 00:34:21,866
these plagues might possibly

636
00:34:21,866 --> 00:34:24,768
even have some connection to the military.

637
00:34:28,040 --> 00:34:29,732
Take what you're seeing now,

638
00:34:29,732 --> 00:34:32,150
for instance — take COVID-19. All along,

639
00:34:32,150 --> 00:34:34,810
from the very start, there have been certain

640
00:34:34,810 --> 00:34:37,348
people who didn't want anyone looking into

641
00:34:37,348 --> 00:34:38,920
where it really came from.

642
00:34:38,920 --> 00:34:40,894
But there's no shortage of evidence

643
00:34:40,894 --> 00:34:43,390
that it likely came from a laboratory.

644
00:34:46,780 --> 00:34:51,192
So you know — conventional weapons, we just talked about some.

645
00:34:51,980 --> 00:34:55,347
Unconventional weapons — besides nuclear,

646
00:34:55,347 --> 00:34:58,133
there are also biological weapons.

647
00:34:58,480 --> 00:35:01,883
This passage talks about sword, famine, plague,

648
00:35:01,883 --> 00:35:04,400
and wild beasts killing a quarter of the earth.

649
00:35:04,400 --> 00:35:06,620
Now, the world has over 8 billion people.

650
00:35:06,620 --> 00:35:09,290
A quarter dying — that's 2 billion people.

651
00:35:10,600 --> 00:35:13,750
China has 1.3 billion, the U.S. just over 300 million.

652
00:35:13,750 --> 00:35:17,592
Even adding up the populations of the U.S. and China —

653
00:35:17,592 --> 00:35:19,780
it still wouldn't reach that figure.

654
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:21,289
Do you understand?

655
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:23,496
Why would so many people die?

656
00:35:23,496 --> 00:35:25,237
The Bible also speaks of

657
00:35:25,237 --> 00:35:27,733
a great fire in that war —

658
00:35:27,733 --> 00:35:31,216
a third of the earth burned, all the grass burned,

659
00:35:31,216 --> 00:35:36,325
a third of the seas struck, a third of the sun, moon, and stars affected.

660
00:35:40,080 --> 00:35:43,942
I don't believe this means the sun and moon themselves have problems —

661
00:35:43,942 --> 00:35:47,980
rather, it's the atmosphere — the atmosphere has been compromised.

662
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:50,999
What could cause a third of the earth, a third of the sea,

663
00:35:50,999 --> 00:35:53,611
and a third of the atmosphere to be affected?

664
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:00,831
I think it's very likely connected to nuclear war.

665
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:02,059
Next week,

666
00:36:02,059 --> 00:36:03,800
we'll talk more about this aspect.

667
00:36:07,220 --> 00:36:07,800
Why?

668
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:11,402
Because you might think — the Bible is the word of God,

669
00:36:11,402 --> 00:36:13,120
but the Bible is very old.

670
00:36:13,120 --> 00:36:15,036
Since the Bible is so ancient,

671
00:36:15,036 --> 00:36:17,474
how could it describe these modern terms?

672
00:36:19,040 --> 00:36:22,871
Understand? These modern situations —

673
00:36:22,871 --> 00:36:24,613
it can actually describe them.

674
00:36:24,740 --> 00:36:27,701
We can dig deeper into this next time.

675
00:36:29,660 --> 00:36:32,546
But at the very least, you'll remember that

676
00:36:32,546 --> 00:36:34,493
back in January and February,

677
00:36:34,493 --> 00:36:37,580
wasn't the U.S. still going on about a certain

678
00:36:37,580 --> 00:36:40,735
topic — this idea of wanting to take Greenland,

679
00:36:40,735 --> 00:36:43,822
in order to complete its so-called Golden Dome

680
00:36:43,822 --> 00:36:44,695
defense plan?

681
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:47,519
Some people might think

682
00:36:47,519 --> 00:36:51,340
nuclear missiles fly east-to-west or west-to-east.

683
00:36:51,340 --> 00:36:55,055
But have you considered — they can also fly northward,

684
00:36:55,055 --> 00:36:58,364
across the North Pole. The route is much shorter.

685
00:37:01,700 --> 00:37:04,893
For example, certain airline routes today —

686
00:37:04,893 --> 00:37:08,085
flight routes — also cross over the Arctic.

687
00:37:10,240 --> 00:37:13,317
So all of this connects back to nuclear weapons.

688
00:37:17,040 --> 00:37:19,619
Recently there have been many such things —

689
00:37:19,619 --> 00:37:25,040
about nuclear war, nuclear missile interception, and so on.

690
00:37:25,040 --> 00:37:27,188
Coming back to that saying:

691
00:37:27,188 --> 00:37:31,367
every weapon humans have invented has eventually been used.

692
00:37:32,500 --> 00:37:34,706
And today's nuclear bombs

693
00:37:34,706 --> 00:37:40,975
are more than a thousand times more powerful than those dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

694
00:37:44,820 --> 00:37:48,245
Alright — what about the killer robots you mentioned?

695
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:51,939
In the Bible — Revelation chapter 9 —

696
00:37:51,939 --> 00:37:53,622
there's a description like this.

697
00:37:55,120 --> 00:37:59,920
It speaks of several angels sounding their trumpets — seven angels in all.

698
00:37:59,920 --> 00:38:01,487
When the sixth angel sounded,

699
00:38:01,487 --> 00:38:04,216
he said a voice came from before God,

700
00:38:04,216 --> 00:38:08,860
from the four horns of the golden altar — and what did it say?

701
00:38:09,120 --> 00:38:11,790
It said to release the four angels

702
00:38:11,790 --> 00:38:14,809
who had been bound at the Euphrates River.

703
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:19,874
And those four angels were released.

704
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:21,091
They had been prepared

705
00:38:21,091 --> 00:38:25,420
for a specific year, month, day, and hour,

706
00:38:25,420 --> 00:38:27,560
to kill a third of mankind.

707
00:38:27,560 --> 00:38:31,217
And the armies of horsemen numbered 200 million —

708
00:38:31,217 --> 00:38:33,017
I heard their number.

709
00:38:33,440 --> 00:38:37,503
The second time massive death is mentioned is right here.

710
00:38:37,800 --> 00:38:41,284
Killing a third of mankind. If you do the math:

711
00:38:41,284 --> 00:38:44,823
first the four horsemen kill a quarter of humanity —

712
00:38:44,823 --> 00:38:49,940
the world goes from 8 billion to 6 billion. 2 billion dead.

713
00:38:49,940 --> 00:38:54,020
Then a third of the remaining 6 billion die — another 2 billion gone.

714
00:38:54,020 --> 00:38:54,949
By this point,

715
00:38:54,949 --> 00:38:57,271
half the world's population is gone.

716
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:02,960
And let me tell you — what point in time is this?

717
00:39:02,960 --> 00:39:07,372
This is only the halfway mark of the seven-year tribulation.

718
00:39:07,660 --> 00:39:11,433
The final disasters haven't even happened yet.

719
00:39:11,640 --> 00:39:14,040
The last three and a half years haven't come.

720
00:39:14,040 --> 00:39:18,120
Speaking of those last three and a half years, what does the Bible say?

721
00:39:18,120 --> 00:39:22,283
It says: if not for the sake of the elect, for the sake of Israel,

722
00:39:22,283 --> 00:39:25,940
if those days were not cut short, no flesh would be saved.

723
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:30,642
So you realize — the disasters in the earlier period,

724
00:39:30,642 --> 00:39:33,603
the scale of the killing is already severe.

725
00:39:35,540 --> 00:39:38,210
And the four seals we just mentioned —

726
00:39:38,210 --> 00:39:42,158
those happen shortly after the seven-year tribulation begins,

727
00:39:42,158 --> 00:39:43,725
after the white horse.

728
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:48,661
And here, when the sixth angel sounds his trumpet —

729
00:39:48,661 --> 00:39:51,157
what does that mean? You could say:

730
00:39:51,157 --> 00:39:52,608
after the four horses,

731
00:39:52,608 --> 00:39:54,756
later there's an Antichrist —

732
00:39:54,756 --> 00:39:57,020
the kingdom of the Beast emerges.

733
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:02,514
People are required to worship him,

734
00:40:02,514 --> 00:40:04,662
and dissenters are slaughtered.

735
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:07,516
A killing of dissenters.

736
00:40:07,640 --> 00:40:10,368
And in this passage, you'll notice —

737
00:40:10,368 --> 00:40:12,690
besides killing a third of humanity,

738
00:40:12,690 --> 00:40:14,722
there are two other striking features.

739
00:40:15,320 --> 00:40:19,740
First, you notice it specifies a particular year, month, day, and hour.

740
00:40:19,740 --> 00:40:22,123
Very curious — it doesn't say where the soldiers come from,

741
00:40:22,123 --> 00:40:24,465
where they're going, what war they're fighting.

742
00:40:24,465 --> 00:40:27,251
Many people confuse this passage

743
00:40:27,251 --> 00:40:29,747
with Armageddon or other events.

744
00:40:30,640 --> 00:40:32,556
But it isn't. Look carefully —

745
00:40:32,556 --> 00:40:34,529
there are significant hints in the text.

746
00:40:35,660 --> 00:40:37,060
What does it say?

747
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:41,820
It says: it originates from four angels released at the Euphrates River.

748
00:40:44,340 --> 00:40:47,100
There's a connection to the spiritual realm.

749
00:40:47,100 --> 00:40:50,989
And you know the Euphrates is in modern-day Iraq —

750
00:40:50,989 --> 00:40:53,660
also the region of ancient Babylon.

751
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,790
Back to that original place of rebellion.

752
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:03,612
And notice this:

753
00:41:03,612 --> 00:41:08,460
a specific year, month, day, and hour, killing a third of mankind.

754
00:41:08,460 --> 00:41:09,215
Do you see it?

755
00:41:10,840 --> 00:41:12,930
It doesn't look like a war.

756
00:41:13,040 --> 00:41:15,940
It looks more like the execution of an order.

757
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:18,552
Like the execution of a command.

758
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:21,159
Recently, Donald Trump

759
00:41:21,159 --> 00:41:23,713
in a national address a few days ago

760
00:41:23,713 --> 00:41:25,455
said something like this:

761
00:41:25,455 --> 00:41:27,370
in modern history,

762
00:41:27,370 --> 00:41:30,563
no war has ever lasted just a single day.

763
00:41:33,760 --> 00:41:39,391
The current Iran war has already gone over thirty days, a whole month.

764
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:44,690
Modern wars never last a single day, let alone half a day.

765
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:49,319
And here it specifies a particular day and a particular hour.

766
00:41:49,319 --> 00:41:52,918
So doesn't it look like the execution of a command?

767
00:41:55,540 --> 00:42:00,184
And finding targets to execute a command — that's very easy.

768
00:42:01,960 --> 00:42:04,859
For example, you may well have seen that recent

769
00:42:04,859 --> 00:42:07,264
piece of news — the one about Facebook,

770
00:42:07,264 --> 00:42:10,040
about Meta, and some of the other platforms —

771
00:42:10,040 --> 00:42:11,767
where the courts fined them,

772
00:42:11,767 --> 00:42:13,864
saying they were using algorithms,

773
00:42:13,864 --> 00:42:16,639
using all that data, to work out exactly what

774
00:42:16,639 --> 00:42:19,476
people like, and then feed them just the right

775
00:42:19,476 --> 00:42:21,203
content to keep them hooked,

776
00:42:21,203 --> 00:42:22,560
to keep them addicted.

777
00:42:22,560 --> 00:42:26,391
Actually, with that kind of data you can know exactly

778
00:42:26,391 --> 00:42:28,191
what people are doing online,

779
00:42:28,191 --> 00:42:30,397
what kind of person they roughly are,

780
00:42:30,397 --> 00:42:33,648
what they like to watch, their personal preferences,

781
00:42:33,648 --> 00:42:35,853
their political leanings, all of it.

782
00:42:39,600 --> 00:42:42,619
So it's very easy to identify targets.

783
00:42:43,900 --> 00:42:45,525
Another point worth mentioning:

784
00:42:45,525 --> 00:42:48,486
it says the cavalry numbered 200 million.

785
00:42:49,360 --> 00:42:53,000
In the world today, which country has a 200-million-strong army?

786
00:42:53,000 --> 00:42:55,032
None. Understand?

787
00:42:56,680 --> 00:42:59,524
But — if you think about

788
00:42:59,524 --> 00:43:02,775
the robots we just talked about, killer robots,

789
00:43:02,775 --> 00:43:06,316
having that many isn't hard at all. Why?

790
00:43:07,740 --> 00:43:11,120
200 million. But is the goal to kill a third?

791
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:12,720
A third would be 2 billion.

792
00:43:12,720 --> 00:43:15,332
One robot to ten people. Understand?

793
00:43:17,100 --> 00:43:18,880
So it's absolutely possible.

794
00:43:18,880 --> 00:43:22,247
And what does that cavalry look like in the description?

795
00:43:23,740 --> 00:43:26,019
In the vision, the riders sit on horses,

796
00:43:26,019 --> 00:43:28,461
wearing breastplates — fire,

797
00:43:28,461 --> 00:43:31,012
breastplates as red as fire,

798
00:43:31,012 --> 00:43:33,400
hyacinth-blue, and sulfur-yellow.

799
00:43:33,400 --> 00:43:36,070
The horses' heads were like lions' heads.

800
00:43:37,400 --> 00:43:39,200
Like lions' heads.

801
00:43:39,300 --> 00:43:42,195
I know everyone's getting hungry — it's lunchtime —

802
00:43:42,195 --> 00:43:45,800
but "lions' heads" here isn't the Shanghai dish.

803
00:43:45,800 --> 00:43:47,541
Fire, smoke, and sulfur

804
00:43:47,541 --> 00:43:49,805
came out of the horses' mouths.

805
00:43:50,880 --> 00:43:54,073
These three things killed a third of mankind.

806
00:43:55,080 --> 00:43:58,447
The horses' power was in their mouths and in their tails.

807
00:43:58,600 --> 00:43:59,761
I don't know what that is.

808
00:44:00,280 --> 00:44:03,879
That's what the Apostle John saw back then.

809
00:44:06,760 --> 00:44:09,890
Personally, as I look around at the way things

810
00:44:09,890 --> 00:44:12,817
are developing right now — this current war

811
00:44:12,817 --> 00:44:15,266
between the U.S., Israel, and Iran —

812
00:44:15,266 --> 00:44:18,329
of course there's still an awful lot we could

813
00:44:18,329 --> 00:44:21,459
take away from it, especially on the political

814
00:44:21,459 --> 00:44:22,480
side of things.

815
00:44:22,480 --> 00:44:25,150
For instance, what it means for Europe,

816
00:44:25,150 --> 00:44:28,230
what it means for NATO, and also for Israel's

817
00:44:28,230 --> 00:44:30,626
whole position in the Middle East —

818
00:44:30,626 --> 00:44:33,843
but all of that we'll have to leave for another

819
00:44:33,843 --> 00:44:35,760
day, when we get the chance.

820
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:39,069
But on the matter of weapons, what can we see?

821
00:44:40,660 --> 00:44:42,924
The U.S. economy, U.S. technology,

822
00:44:42,924 --> 00:44:45,943
U.S. military power are all formidable.

823
00:44:46,760 --> 00:44:49,256
If you wanted to take on the United States,

824
00:44:49,256 --> 00:44:53,784
it seems the only option would be nuclear weapons.

825
00:44:56,940 --> 00:45:01,294
And when the U.S. and the rival powers all step down together,

826
00:45:01,294 --> 00:45:04,138
you find there aren't many remaining powers.

827
00:45:07,000 --> 00:45:09,264
Maybe just Europe. Understand?

828
00:45:10,240 --> 00:45:13,665
And when that happens, a hostile power will arise —

829
00:45:13,665 --> 00:45:16,393
the kingdom of the Antichrist will appear.

830
00:45:17,960 --> 00:45:20,787
He'll demand worship; refuse and you die.

831
00:45:20,787 --> 00:45:24,154
That's the scene described under the sixth trumpet.

832
00:45:24,860 --> 00:45:27,182
I think the weapons part of the picture —

833
00:45:27,182 --> 00:45:29,330
you can already see the early signs.

834
00:45:31,960 --> 00:45:33,760
So what lies ahead?

835
00:45:35,740 --> 00:45:38,991
Today I've tried — through the lens of weapons —

836
00:45:38,991 --> 00:45:41,603
to tell you about this war.

837
00:45:42,380 --> 00:45:43,019
Next week,

838
00:45:43,019 --> 00:45:45,050
we'll talk about what's ahead.

839
00:45:49,020 --> 00:45:52,213
That's not just current-events analysis —

840
00:45:52,213 --> 00:45:53,548
in the Bible,

841
00:45:53,548 --> 00:45:55,522
how does it describe what's coming?

842
00:45:55,920 --> 00:45:58,320
We'll see each other again next week, okay?

843
00:45:58,320 --> 00:46:00,020
That's all I have for today.

